# Patch:        020-budget-binding-index
# Phase:        pre
# Target:       security/src/budget/report.rs
# Hunks:        2
# Class:        equiv-rewrite
# Gap:          docs/extractor-issues.md 18.
#               aeneas rejects a loop that reassigns a borrow-carrying value.
#               It also rejects a loop that runs while such a value is borrowed.
# Cost:         None in a normal build, where the two new functions are compiled but unused.
#               In the extracted variant, the binding term is read at the first minimum.
#               That minimum is taken over the seven bit counts.
# Upstreamable: no: the term-copying loop is the natural Rust.
# Drop when:    aeneas supports loops over borrow-carrying values.
# Test:         test p3-security --lib pre_patch_020
# Mutation:     The first-minimum loop keeps the last minimum on a tie.
#               It then disagrees with the binding term, and the differential test fails.
#
# The binding term is found by a loop that keeps a running term and reassigns it.
# A term holds a `&'static str`, so aeneas cannot translate that loop.
#
# A new function runs the same loop over the seven `u64` bit counts.
# A second new function feeds it those counts.
# Both are compiled in every build.
# Under cfg(hax_backend_lean), the binding term is the term at the returned index.
#
# Both selections resolve a tie to the earliest round.
# The differential test compares the two on 20000 reports.
# Half of the reports use values in 0..4, to force ties.
#
